It holds that
\begin{multline*}
|\cost[t](X,C)-\cost[t](D,C)|\leq |\cost(X_{A_1},C)-\cost(D_{A_1},C)| \\ +\int_{(r^\ast-2r)^+}^{r^\ast+2r} \left\lvert  |B(C,u)\cap X_{A_3}| - \left\lVert B(C,u)\cap D_{A_3} \right\rVert_1  \right\rvert du.
\end{multline*}

With probability $1-O\big(\frac{1}{\log(k/\varepsilon)\cdot \log (mk)}\big)$, $U$ is an $\varepsilon$-indexed-subset cost approximation, i.e., it holds for every $J\subseteq [k_0]$ and every $C\in \binom{M}{k}$ that
\begin{equation} 
    \left\lvert  \cost(U_J,C)-\cost(X_J,C)  \right\rvert \leq \varepsilon\cdot \cost(X_J,C)+\varepsilon n r.
\end{equation}

With probability at least $1-O\big(\frac{1}{\log \frac{k}{\varepsilon}\cdot \log (mk)}\big)$, the following inequality holds for every $J\subseteq [k_0]$, $C\in \binom{M}{k}$, and each $r>0$:
\begin{equation} 
\left\lvert  |B(C,r)\cap X_J| - \left\lVert B(C,r)\cap U_J \right\rVert_1  \right\rvert\leq \varepsilon n.
\end{equation}
